  "question": {
    "text": "Two masses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;are connected by a string of length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>l</mi></math>. They are held in a horizontal plane at a height&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>H</mi></math>&nbsp;above two heavy plates&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math> made of different materials placed on the floor. Initially, the distance between the two masses is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>l</mi></math>. When the masses are released they fall freely and collide with the plates simultaneously. The coefficient of restitution for the collision of the first ball-plate pair is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>8</mn></math>&nbsp;and for the second ball-plate pair is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>4</mn></math>. The time after the collision when the string becomes taut is<br />\r<br />\r<img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\qt0018_e194a9c9a21e.jpg\" style=\"width: 180px; height: 130px;\" />"
  },
